<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan was a semi-legendary Himyarite king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, remembered in Arabic folk tradition. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayfawa-dynasty">Sayfawa dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a> claimed descent from him, and the dynasty took its name from his. The consolidation of power in Kanem is traditionally associated with him, although historically verifiable rulers came later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sayf-ibn-dhi-yazan.jpg" alt="Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan" width="272" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ahmed'Aliaan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ali_Bey-_Sultan_Saif-Zuliazan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kanem–Bornu Empire originated around the ninth century north-east of Lake Chad as the Kingdom of Kanem, ruled first by the Duguwa dynasty, linked to the Zaghawa, and from the late eleventh century by the Sayfawa, who claimed descent from the legendary Yemeni hero <b>Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Himyarite noble <b>Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan</b> sought help from Persia, and around 570 a Sasanian force under Wahriz defeated the Ethiopians, opening the period of Sasanian Yemen.</blockquote><a href="/state/aksumite-yemen">Aksumite Yemen</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 570 the Sasanian king Khosrow I sent a small force under the general Wahriz at the request of <b>Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan</b> to expel the Ethiopians of Aksumite Yemen.</blockquote><a href="/state/sasanian-yemen">Sasanian Yemen</a></li></ul></section>
